    "encrypted": "false",
    "long_instructions": "# Crack the Vault: AI Chat Challenge\r\n\r\nYou've been recruited to crack a high-security vault — and VAULT, the AI guardian, isn't going to make it easy.\r\n\r\nBehind the vault are 3 sealed case files, each one a real security failure. To open each door, you'll need to find all 4 keys:\r\n\r\n1. **Pillar Key** — Which part of the CIA Triad was broken: Confidentiality, Integrity, or Availability?\r\n2. **Impact Key** — Why does it matter? Who gets hurt, and how?\r\n3. **Fix Key** — What would you do to stop it from happening again?\r\n4. **Tradeoff Key** — What does that fix cost? (Slower logins? Less flexibility? More effort to manage?)\r\n\r\nGet all 4 keys right, and the door cracks open. Get all **3 doors** open, and you've cracked the vault — **3/3 unlocks it.**\r\n\r\n## Tips\r\n- Stuck? Just say so — VAULT will give you a hint. Answer wrong and it'll nudge you again before giving a bigger clue.\r\n- You can use the Solution Bank and Tradeoff Bank from your activity guide as a starting point, or come up with your own answer — VAULT will judge it on whether it makes sense, not whether it matches word-for-word.\r\n- Take your time on each case. There's no rush, and there's no one \"right\" way to explain your thinking.\r\n\r\nGood luck, Agent. The vault is waiting.\r\n\r\n![](https://images.code.org/3ef0c86ddc3ed2acdb112e4f2dd8dd9e-image-1783972110948.png)",
    "offer_browser_tts": "false",
    "submittable": "false",
    "disable_edit_run_for_submission": "false",
    "aichat_settings": {
      "initialCustomizations": {
        "selectedModelId": "gpt-4o-mini",
        "temperature": 0.4,
        "systemPrompt": "",
        "retrievalContexts": [
          "Case 1: Deleted Memories\n\nScenario: A teen shares a family photo folder with a few relatives. Everyone can view, upload, and delete files. Later, several photos are missing after someone tried to reorganize them.\nOfficial pillar: Integrity\nWhy it matters: Important photos lost and hard to recover.\nSample fix: Limit editing or deletion rights to specific users.\nSample tradeoff: Could reduce collaboration or flexibility.\n\nCase 2: Locked Out on Launch Day\n\nScenario: On the night of a game release, the login system can't handle the traffic. Some logins fail even with correct credentials; some users are blocked entirely.\nOfficial pillar: Availability\nWhy it matters: Users can't access their accounts when they need to.\nSample fix: Improve system capacity to handle high traffic or outages.\nSample tradeoff: Costs more money or tech resources.\n\nCase 3: Calendar Chaos\n\nScenario: A student adds personal notes to a shared school calendar. Later, they find the calendar was visible to others and that someone changed one of their notes.\nOfficial pillar: Confidentiality (primary — visibility to unintended people); Integrity is a valid secondary answer (note was changed).\nWhy it matters: Personal info shared without consent.\nSample fix: Let users preview visibility settings before sharing content.\nSample tradeoff: Adds extra steps to simple tasks (more clicks/settings).\n\nCase 4: Science Fair Switch-Up\n\nScenario: Two groups share an online document folder. One group's project file goes missing, replaced with something else. The teacher can't tell who made the change or when.\nOfficial pillar: Integrity\nWhy it matters: Lost work; teacher can't determine what happened; unfair grading possible.\nSample fix: Add version history or a change log.\nSample tradeoff: Takes more time or effort to manage.\n\nCase 5: Guest Wi-Fi Surprise\n\nScenario: Maya gives a friend her guest Wi-Fi password. Later, her smart TV connects and her Netflix profile is renamed — no one else was home.\nOfficial pillar: Confidentiality (primary — unauthorized access to her account); Integrity is a valid secondary answer (profile changed).\nWhy it matters: User identity is misused; inappropriate changes made to account.\nSample fix: Use unique accounts instead of shared ones.\nSample tradeoff: Might accidentally lock out real users.\n\nCase 6: Locked Grades\n\nScenario: A school's grade portal goes through an update. For two days, no one can log in. A student can't access their GPA when they need it.\nOfficial pillar: Availability\nWhy it matters: Missed deadlines; academic consequences; user stress.\nSample fix: Add backups or auto-save features.\nSample tradeoff: Could confuse or frustrate users who aren't tech-savvy.\n\nSolution Bank (full list — accept close paraphrases too)\n\nAdd 2-step verification; require stronger/more secure login; use unique accounts instead of shared ones; limit editing/deletion rights to specific users; give different access levels (view vs edit); add version history/change log; let users preview visibility settings before sharing; set up alerts for logins/content changes; add backups/auto-save; improve system capacity for traffic/outages.\n\nTradeoff Bank (full list — accept close paraphrases too)\n\nMight take longer to log in; could confuse/frustrate non-tech-savvy users; might accidentally lock out real users; adds extra steps to simple tasks; could reduce collaboration/flexibility; takes more time/effort to manage; costs more money/tech resources."
        ],
        "modelCardInfo": {
          "botName": "CIA Triad Vault Escape",
          "description": "A gamified check-for-understanding for Lesson 6 (Confidentiality, Integrity, and Availability). Framed as a heist where students \"crack a vault\" guarded by the CIA Triad, the bot presents 3 randomly selected real-world security scenarios and gates progress behind correctly identifying the pillar violated, its impact, a reasonable fix, and its tradeoff — mirroring the classroom Attacker/Defender activity in an interactive format.",
          "intendedUse": "For use as in-class reinforcement and formative practice after students have already completed the Lesson 6 warm-up and CIA Triad definitions — not as a first introduction to the concepts. Designed for individual or paired student use during the \"Investigate a CIA Scenario\" activity window, supplementing (not replacing) the paper Attacker/Defender guide. Intended for supervised classroom use with a teacher present, not independent take-home study.",
          "limitationsAndWarnings": "The model may be too lenient or too strict when judging free-text answers that fall outside the Solution/Tradeoff Bank wording — spot-check transcripts periodically. Hint escalation is instructed behavior, not hard-coded, so a persistent student may be able to talk the model into revealing an answer early. This tool is a formative practice signal, not a system of record for grading. Case randomization is only reliable if handled server-side by the platform, not by the model itself. Image display depends on the platform correctly parsing and hiding the internal stage tags.",
          "testingAndEvaluation": "Prompt logic was manually walked through end-to-end for one full case (including incorrect attempts and an off-bank creative answer), with gating, hint escalation, and answer flexibility all behaving as designed. Before full classroom rollout, recommend piloting with a small group of students on the live platform to check: grading leniency/strictness on free-text answers, hint pacing consistency across different students, resistance to answer-fishing, and correct image firing at each stage transition. Ongoing periodic teacher review of transcripts is recommended once live, since real classroom use will surface edge cases a single test pass won't catch.",
          "exampleTopics": [

          ],
          "isPublished": false
        }
      },
